hat we can google  also preferences giving customers choice  we want to be able to provide an array  of offerings so that customers can  choose which different pieces are going  to fit their solutions best and Google  first and for  most is committed to modernization and  to an open platform we are devoted to  the open source community and we are the  innovators behind things like kubernetes  tensorflow gke and other tools that are  commonly used today throughout a variety  of clouds so let's talk a little bit  about our three staged approach first is  to migrate so we help you with the lift  and shift moving from your on-premises  data center into Google Cloud migrating  your VMs into our Google compute engine  and potentially even upgrading those  versions of the operating system where  possible I'll talk a little bit more  about that in a moment this is using our  migrate for compute engine tool by doing  this we work with you to get the right  balance of your bring your own licensing  capabilities versus that pay-as-you-go  licensing we help you take advantage of  Google technology such as CPU over  commit and custom VM shapes and we allow  you to leverage suspending and resuming  your VMs so that you can save on costs  when things aren't actually needed next  we'll talk a little bit about our  modernization opportunities these are  the ways in which we can help you bring  your Windows applications into  containers and still be in Windows or  taking advantage of our fully managed  service offerings like cloud sequel or  managed Active Directory and all of this  results in a greater efficiency tighter  bin packing and better cost optimization  lastly we can talk about how we can help  you transform your applications moving  to more open standards like Postgres or  my sequel or moving your dotnet on  windows into dotnet core or lastly  taking your dotnet applications in  container izing them for ease of  management and ease of scale where do I  go  as I said Google wants to provide you a  variety of choices so that you can  choose per workload what the best  destination is for your Windows  applications first choice is the Google  compute engine this is our standard  Google VM environment where we can help  you bring your on-prem VMs using our  migrate for compute engine  if there are applications that it makes  more sense to stay in vmware we can help  you stay in vm we are with our vmware as  a service offering allows you to  leverage native vmware tools like hcx to  do your migrations and helps you move  those workloads from vmware on-premises  into vmware in google cloud where you  can still take advantage of all of the  Google cloud features and services  lastly for those items that are  appropriate to containerize we can help  you with our migrated 4 anthos tool to  take your dotnet applications that are  running in VMs today and actually put  them into windows containers running in  kubernetes similarly for Microsoft  sequel you have the opportunity to  continue running your own Microsoft  sequel on Windows in our Google compute  engine in a virtual machine or we can  help you migrate to sequel for Linux  thus eliminating the need to pay for the  underlying operating system of Windows  because Linux is free or if you want to  get out of the business of managing your  own sequel server we can help you move  to cloud sequel where we need words a  fully managed service offering and you  just need to tap in so a little bit of  an overview on licensing with a quick  disclaimer this is guidance at best  please make sure that you check the  details of your individual Microsoft  licensing so essentially there are two  models for how you can take care of your  microsoft licenses within Google Cloud  the first is very easy and  straightforward is just pay-as-you-go if  you'd like to deploy a Microsoft Windows  server in Google cloud when you're  creating that VM you select the windows  server image and that image will not  only