 optimal amount of RAM for your  sequel server or other applications  while still not having to overpay for  CPUs that you don't actually need  Windows Server 2008 is another topic  that we frequently hear from our  customers  Windows Server 2008 r2 has gone  end-of-life which means that Microsoft  will no longer provide security updates  or patches for it without having to  purchase potentially expensive extended  security updates migrate for compute  engine allows you to take those 2008 r2  servers from your data center and in the  process of migration actually upgrade  them in flight so that when they land in  GCE they land as 2012 servers which is a  fully supported operating system that is  provided at no cost to our customers by  using the different tools and technology  that Google provides optimizing your per  second billing being able to shape the  custom VMs taking advantage of  preemptable VMs so for those workloads  that just need episodic access to  compute you can spin them up and spin  them down at a greatly reduced price we  also have an ongoing recommendation  system where you can see at a VM by VM  level what the recommendations are for  scaling up or scaling down based on your  actual utilization of those VMs  allowing you to right-size your spend  and right-size your utilization of  resources let's talk a little bit now  about modernizing so some of the ways  that we help you modernize around sequel  by moving to sequel on Linux for example  you remove the underlying need to  license the Windows operating system by  moving to manage sequel you don't have  to pay the sequel license costs at all  because all of that is managed within  our cloud sequel offering and by moving  that responsibility of management we may  even be able to help you move from  enterprise to standard to web and as you  can see moving from server Enterprise to  sitt sequel server standard can be a  tremendous cost savings being able to  move all the way to sequel server web  